WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.079 --> 00:00:01.440
[Music starts] [Audio Description - not captioned]

00:00:01.473 --> 00:00:02.826
[Jeremy Weiss, PhD, MD] I lead the CHARM lab.

00:00:02.826 --> 00:00:07.948
It's the Care Health And Reasoning Machine
Lab. And we develop machine learning tools

00:00:14.989 --> 00:00:21.750
to better utilize electronic health records
data for patient characterization and for

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:22.934
risk assessment.

00:00:24.301 --> 00:00:29.060
Part of my work focuses on longitudinal data
that comes from electronic health records

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:34.845
so that we can do forecasting, so that we
can know a patient's risk for particular sequelae

00:00:34.845 --> 00:00:39.620
of disease forward in time, or we can at least
estimate it or predict it.

00:00:41.485 --> 00:00:48.157
Another part of my work focuses on Equitable
Risk Assessments so that the models that we

00:00:48.157 --> 00:00:52.662
develop have performance characteristics across
different subgroups

00:00:59.085 --> 00:01:00.549
that may be of interest.

00:01:00.549 --> 00:01:05.666
We don't want the models to underperform in
any particular subgroup.

00:01:06.686 --> 00:01:12.770
A third line of work is to better understand
electronic health records data because electronic

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:17.136
health records data are a little bit different
than other types of clinical data

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:22.220
in that they're passively collected.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:26.530
They're collected by indication as in the
provider has to seek out that information

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:28.930
for it to be entered into the electronic health
record.

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:32.677
Whether or not something is there is makes
the data incomplete.

00:01:34.209 --> 00:01:37.261
Not every patient is going to have every measurement
collected.

00:01:37.261 --> 00:01:42.349
And so, we try to understand how do we build
models that capture this type of data irregularity

00:01:44.942 --> 00:01:49.568
and be able to use it for prediction and for
characterization purposes.

00:01:50.647 --> 00:01:57.527
I think that better healthcare going forward
is data-based policy, data-based healthcare.

00:01:57.527 --> 00:02:04.235
And that's why we need to develop tools and
methods that help utilize that data the best

00:02:04.235 --> 00:02:08.104
we can so that we can make the most informed
decisions for patients.

00:02:08.393 --> 00:02:11.673
[Music]

00:02:11.673 --> 00:02:14.311
[Music fades to silence]

